class Solution {
    public ArrayList<Integer> kLargest(int[] arr, int k) {

        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();      // min-heap, smallest on top

        for(int x : arr){
            pq.add(x);                                          // push every element

            if(pq.size() > k)                                   // heap crossed size k
                pq.poll();                                      // drop the smallest
        }

        ArrayList<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();

        while(!pq.isEmpty())
            ans.add(pq.poll());                                 // comes out in increasing order

        Collections.reverse(ans);                               // make it decreasing

        return ans;
    }
}

//////////////////////// DIAGRAM /////////////////////////////

FLOW

  arr --> add x --> size > k ? --yes--> poll smallest --+
              ^           |                             |
              |           no                            |
              +-----------+-----------------------------+

  after loop: heap holds the k largest
  poll all --> increasing --> reverse --> decreasing

HEAP RULE (min-heap)

        small
        /   \
     big     big        root is always the smallest, so it is the one kicked out

